\\(TQ = TR\\), point \\(T\\) must be the midpoint of \\(QR\\). Therefore, \\(T\\) divides \\(QR\\) into two equal segments, each of length \\(w/2\\).\n\n4. **Calculate the area of triangle \\(PQT\\):**\n   Since \\(T\\) is the midpoint of \\(QR\\), the height of triangle \\(PQT\\) is \\(w/2\\) and the base is \\(l\\). The area of triangle \\(PQT\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } \\triangle PQT = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times l \\times \\frac{w}{2} = \\frac{1}{4} \\times l \\times w\n   \\]\n   Substituting the area of the rectangle:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } \\triangle PQT = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 24 = 6\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the area of quadrilateral \\(PTRS\\):**\n   The area of quadrilateral \\(PTRS\\) can be found by subtracting the area of triangle \\(PQT\\) from the area of rectangle \\(PQRS\\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } PTRS = \\text{Area of } PQRS - \\text{Area of } \\triangle PQT = 24 - 6 = 18\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of quadrilateral \\(PTRS\\) is \\(\\boxed{18}\\)."},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{18}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true,"retains_student_answer":false},"graded_text":"To find the area of the quadrilateral \\(PTRS\\), we first need to understand the given information and the geometric relationships in the diagram.\n\n1. **Identify the area of rectangle \\(PQRS\\):**\n   The area of rectangle \\(PQRS\\) is given as 24.\n\n2. **Define the dimensions of rectangle \\(PQRS\\):**\n   Let the length of \\(PQ\\) be \\(l\\) and the width of \\(PQ\\) be \\(w\\). Therefore, the area of rectangle \\(PQRS\\) can be expressed as:\n   \\[\n   l \\times w = 24\n   \\]\n\n3. **Understand the point \\(T\\):**\n   Point \\(T\\) is such that \\(TQ = TR\\). Since \\(T\\) lies on the line segment \\(QR\\), and \\(TQ = TR\\), point \\(T\\) must be the midpoint of \\(QR\\). Therefore, \\(T\\) divides \\(QR\\) into two equal segments, each of length \\(w/2\\).\n\n4. **Calculate the area of triangle \\(PQT\\):**\n   Since \\(T\\) is the midpoint of \\(QR\\), the height of triangle \\(PQT\\) is \\(w/2\\) and the base is \\(l\\). The area of triangle \\(PQT\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } \\triangle PQT = \\frac{1}{2} \\times \\text{base} \\times \\text{height} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times l \\times \\frac{w}{2} = \\frac{1}{4} \\times l \\times w\n   \\]\n   Substituting the area of the rectangle:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } \\triangle PQT = \\frac{1}{4} \\times 24 = 6\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the area of quadrilateral \\(PTRS\\):**\n   The area of quadrilateral \\(PTRS\\) can be found by subtracting the area of triangle \\(PQT\\) from the area of rectangle \\(PQRS\\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area of } PTRS = \\text{Area of } PQRS - \\text{Area of } \\triangle PQT = 24 - 6 = 18\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the area of quadrilateral \\(PTRS\\) is \\(\\boxed{18}\\).","id":"prealgebra:310"}
